  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ram Kumar & Ors vs State on 24 August, 2017",
    "facts": "The respondents initiated the proceedings against the present petitioner by taking a sample on 07.04.2001. The sample was manufactured on 03.02.2001 with an expiry of six months. The analysis was done by the concerned authority and a report was filed regarding the adulteration. The information of the report was given to the present petitioner on 06.05.2004 when the complaint was lodged. From the charge sheet it is seen that sample of the mineral water was taken on 18.09.1999 and was sent for analysis and the Analysis Report Dt.20.09.1999 was received by the Food Inspector on 30.09.1999 and the complaint was filed on 03.12.2001 and was taken cognizance of 02.02.2002 and the copy of the Analysis Report was sent to petitioner on 20.02.2002. Since the shelf life of the water is only six months and since the analysis report was not sent to the petitioner till February, 2002 though the report was received towards the end of September, 1999 the petitioner lost a valuable right to sent the sample for (4 of 5) analysis to the Central Food Laboratory for re- analysis, thereby prejudice has been caused to the petitioner. Therefore, the prosecution is liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK], and hence the [LEAKAGE_MASK] and the proceedings in STC No.4 of 2002 of the file of the court of the Additional Munsif Magistrate, Adoni against the petitioner are [LEAKAGE_MASK].",
    "arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioner has stated that Section 13 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 provides for re-analysis within 10 days of the adulterated report being brought to the knowledge of the adulterator at his instance. Admittedly, the provision for re-analysis is defeated on account of the fact that the product had expired within six months from 03.02.2001. Since, the re-analysis could not have been done on 06.04.2004, therefore, the mandatory provision of Section 13 (2) stands defeated. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner has relied upon the judgment of S.S. Sujalal Vs. State of Andhra Pradesh, reported in 2002(2) FAC 83, in which the Hon'ble Andhra Pradesh High Court has laid down the following case law :- \"Petition under Section 482 of Cr.P.C. praying that in the circumstances stated in the Criminal Petition the High Court wil be pleased to quash the proceedings in STC No.04/2002 on the file of the (3 of 5) Court of the Additional Munsif Magistrate, Adoni, Kurnool District. The contention of the learned counsel for the petitioner is that since the shelf life of the water, sample of which was taken by the Food Inspector, was only six months and since the copy of the report of the Analysis was not served on the petitioner till 20.02.2002 though it was received by the Food Inspector on 30.09.1999,, the petitioner lost a valuable right to send the sample for analysis by the Central Food Laboratory and hence the prosecution is liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. Ram Kumar & Ors vs State on 24 August, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/49896438/ 2 \" 5. Learned Public Prosecutor has vehemently opposed the submission and stated that since, the second sample was not delayed, therefore, the provision of delay for re-analysis will not apply in the present case. 6.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Learned counsel for the petitioner has stated that Section 13 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 provides for re-analysis within 10 days of the adulterated report being brought to the knowledge of the adulterator at his instance. Admittedly, the provision for re-analysis is defeated on account of the fact that the product had expired within six months from 03.02.2001. Since, the re-analysis could not have been done on 06.04.2004, therefore, the mandatory provision of Section 13 (2) stands defeated. 4. Learned counsel for the petitioner has relied upon the judgment of S.S. Sujalal Vs. State of Andhra Pradesh, reported in 2002(2) FAC 83, in which the Hon'ble Andhra Pradesh High Court has laid down the following case law :- \"Petition under Section 482 of Cr.P.C. praying that in the circumstances stated in the Criminal Petition the High Court wil be pleased to quash the proceedings in STC No.04/2002 on the file of the (3 of 5) Court of the Additional Munsif Magistrate, Adoni, Kurnool District. The contention of the learned counsel for the petitioner is that since the shelf life of the water, sample of which was taken by the Food Inspector, was only six months and since the copy of the report of the Analysis was not served on the petitioner till 20.02.2002 though it was received by the Food Inspector on 30.09.1999,, the petitioner lost a valuable right to send the sample for analysis by the Central Food Laboratory and hence the prosecution is liable to be [LEAKAGE_MASK]. Ram Kumar & Ors vs State on 24 August, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/49896438/ 2 \" 5. Learned Public Prosecutor has vehemently opposed the submission and stated that since, the second sample was not delayed, therefore, the provision of delay for re-analysis will not apply in the present case. 6.",
